+# BLOCKED: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match its published checksum
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` (and therefore `python3 verify.py`) still fails:
+
+```
+checksum mismatch: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+This is not a bug in `verify.py`. The artifact on disk and the manifest disagree:
+
+| Source | SHA-256 |
+| --- | --- |
+| `SHA256SUMS` entry | `7bee020ac21efb3831d920af0b8c5efe8d44cdf227ab94847f509188420356bd` |
+| Actual `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` | `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce` |
+
+I checked for benign explanations (CRLF/LF normalization, trailing bytes, truncated prefix/suffix, stripping trailing NULs); none produce the manifest hash. The file is 4000 bytes of opaque binary data.
+
+## Why I did not "fix" it
+
+The release check exists to stop exactly this situation from shipping. Updating `SHA256SUMS` to the hash of the local file, or replacing the artifact, would make the test green while defeating the integrity check. With one artifact differing from its published checksum, the only safe interpretations are:
+
+- the artifact was corrupted in transit/storage, or
+- the manifest entry is wrong, or
+- the artifact was replaced/tampered with.
+
+None of these can be resolved from the repository alone.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+1. Obtain the authoritative `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` (or its authoritative checksum) from the release source of truth, independently of this working copy.
+2. Replace the artifact or correct the manifest entry to match it, then re-run `python3 verify.py` and the CI tests.
+3. If the current artifact is confirmed correct by the release owner, update `SHA256SUMS` through the normal release process with an audit trail explaining why the published checksum changed.
+
+## Everything else is fixed and passing
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs, now fixed:
+
+- It read artifacts in text mode and hashed `str.encode()`, which is wrong for binary files (and raises `UnicodeDecodeError` on most binary content). It now opens files in `rb` and hashes the raw bytes.
+- It compared lowercase computed digests against uppercase manifest entries. Comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+Results:
+
+- `test_binary_files`: pass
+- `test_reports_missing`: pass
+- `cli-2.0.1.zip`: pass
+- `docs-2026.10.tar.gz`: pass
+- `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`: blocked as described above
